        "question": {
          ":question/id": "eecb3474-cb91-5709-adeb-d6b4d01616e3",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
          ":question/problem": "![](/tmp/ca9837-templates-703c6brn/17592186172088/field-b30dac68ff2e00ec20545bd39dd57c3e96fa738ce768729623a416551094336b.png)\n\nCompare the arrows with the positive directions of the labeled axes. All listed formulas are constant vector fields. Which of the following could generate the field shown?\n\nArrow lengths use a common display scale; no numerical magnitude is specified.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "The arrows are parallel to the $z$-axis and point in its **negative** direction. They have the same nonzero length throughout the diagram. A matching field must therefore have zero components on the other axes and a constant negative $\\mathbf k$ component.\n\n- $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf k$ is constant and has the observed axis and direction, so it fits.\n- $\\mathbf G(x,y,z)=\\mathbf k$ points in the opposite direction.\n- $\\mathbf K(x,y,z)=\\mathbf j$ has a nonzero component on another axis, so its arrows have the wrong direction.\n- $\\mathbf H(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf j$ has a nonzero component on another axis, so its arrows have the wrong direction.\n\nTherefore $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf k$ could generate the field.",
